  "accuracyFacts": [
    "ConcurrentDictionary.GetOrAdd and AddOrUpdate keep dictionary operations thread-safe, but value factories can execute more than once and outside internal locks; factory side effects are not an exactly-once operation. AddOrUpdate is not atomic with respect to all other operations on the dictionary.",
    "ConcurrentDictionary.GetEnumerator is safe to use concurrently with reads and writes, but is not a moment-in-time snapshot; it can expose modifications made after enumeration starts.",
    "Thread safety protects collection operations according to each type's contract; it does not automatically make mutable objects stored as keys or values thread-safe, nor does it make multiple calls an atomic transaction.",
    "FrozenDictionary and FrozenSet target read-heavy collections whose contents are established before lookup. They may improve lookup for a workload, but no universal fastest-collection guarantee exists; construction time, memory, key distribution, and workload should be measured.",
    "Immutable collections preserve prior collection structure when updated; structural sharing means an update does not necessarily copy the entire collection. Repeated updates have collection-specific costs; builders can help with batch construction.",
    "A hash-based key or set element must keep equality/hash behavior stable while stored. Changing state used by equality or hashing can make lookup or removal fail; describe this as a violated collection invariant, not general memory corruption.",
    "SortedSet<T> uses its comparer to determine ordering and uniqueness; values for which the comparer returns zero are treated as equivalent for set membership.",
    "List<T>.Sort and Array.Sort are unstable sorts. Do not claim they preserve the relative order of elements that compare equal.",
